WEBVTT

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:06.000
So today we're looking at the pig heart

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.000
and the first thing to do is be able to

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:10.000
tell the anterior surface from the

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:12.000
posterior

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:14.000
surface. You can kind of tell there's a

00:00:14.000 --> 00:00:17.000
jumble of vessels on the back, but what I

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:19.000
always use to help me make sure I'm

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.000
looking at the front is I find the

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:24.000
pulmonary trunk, which has been dissected

00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:26.000
on this one, but you can see it has a

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:28.000
slightly different coloration to it, and

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.000
then that will orient me that, okay, this

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.000
is the front of the pig heart. This is

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:34.000
the pulmonary trunk, therefore this must

00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:37.000
be the right ventricle, and then this

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:40.000
over here must be the left ventricle.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:43.000
Dividing the left and right ventricle,

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.000
you can see a sulcus right here. It's a

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.000
little groove. We call this the

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.000
interventricular sulcus, and this is

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.000
where the left anterior descending

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.000
artery flows, and if someone has a heart

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.000
attack, the probability is greatest that

00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:03.000
this is the vessel that is indeed

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.000
clogged. Okay, so then we'll look up a

00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:05.037
little bit higher and we can find the
